Future :

Future object is the object that stores the output and status of the asynchronous computation that may not give the response immediately.

-> Future object is used to get the status and answer of the asynchronous computation.
-> Using the Future object, we can make use of different methods in order to perform different operations ontop of future object.
-> cancel(boolean ifrunning)-> cancels the asynchronous computation. 
Here the boolean value is true, then the cancel operattion will interupt the asynchronous communication and that thread get interupted only if it responds.
If it is false, then it does not interupt the methods which are already running. But it will cancels the asynchronous computation which is not yet started.
-> isCancelled() return true, if the cancel method is called, no matter what the thread is interupted or not.
No callbacks: You can’t attach a “when complete, do X” without blocking or polling.
-> It wraps the exceptions in the ExecutionException exception in future.get() call only.
Future States
===========================
1. Running
2. Completed
3. Failed (ExecutionException)
4. Cancelled

DrawBacks of Future Object:
============================
Manual composition: Combining results from multiple futures is clumsy.
Error handling is via checked exceptions (ExecutionException, InterruptedException) and requires boilerplate.
No chaining: You can’t easily say “on result of A, start B, then C”.

In order to acheive all these things make use of completable future.

CompletableFuture:
=================
You want non-blocking callbacks (thenApply, thenCompose, thenAccept).
You need pipelines, compositions, parallel joins (allOf, anyOf).
Better exception handling (exceptionally, handle).
Optional custom executors and async stages (supplyAsync, runAsync).

=================================================================================================================
Definition: A handle to an async computation; provides get, cancel, status checks.
Blocking: get() blocks; use timeouts to avoid indefinite waits.
Exceptions: get() wraps task failures in ExecutionException.
Cancellation: Works only if task checks interrupts; cancel(true) attempts to interrupt.
Multiple tasks: Use invokeAll for batch submission and collective completion.
Limitations: No callbacks/composition → prefer CompletableFuture for advanced workflows.
Thread safety: Future methods are thread-safe; the task must manage its own concurrency and interrupt handling.
=================================================================================================================

ExecutorFramework:

1. Executor:
Root interface in the executor framework.
-> Simplest abstract for execution of task
Key Points:
----------
1. No Return Type
2. No Lifecycle maintainance
3. No Exception Handling.
4. It just executes a simple runnable.
5. No shutdown.
6. No Future Object.
7. It contains only 1 abstract method that is execute().

When to use Executor?
1. Simply fire and forget Applications.
2. Rarely used in Real time Applications.


2. ExecutorService:
------------------
1. ExecutorService extends the Executor interface. It provides various methods 
2. Task lifecycle - shutdown, shutdownnow, awaittermination.
3. Result handling (Future Objecy)
4. Shutdown control 
5. Batch execution.(Invoke All, Invoke Any-> if any of the runnable or callable succeeds, then it returns the results).


Methods:
-------
1.submit(Runnable runnable), submit(Callable callable).
2. shutdown(), shutdownNow(), awaitTermination().
3. Future object-> cancel(boolean), isCancelled, get()-status, result, exception wrapped in ExecutionException, isDone().


ScheduledExecutorService:
--------------------------
If you want to execute a task at regular intervals or with a fixed delay asynchronously, we go with scheduled executorservice.
ScheduledExecutorService scheduler =ExecutorService.new ScheduleThreadPool(1);
1. scheduler.schedule(()->System.out.println("Hello after 5 seconds delay"),5, TimeUnits.Seconds);

scheduler.shutdown()-> shuts down at 5 seconds delay..

2. scheduler.scheduleAtFixRate(()-> System.out.println("Task Executed after 5 second delay"),5,6,TimeUnits.SECONDS); 5==initial delay, 6=fixed period irrespective of completion of the tasks. these will be overlapping with the previous tasks.

These will be running continuosly, hence we use scheduler.scheudle((()->scheduler.shutdown),20, TimeUnit.SECONDS)-->Used to stop the tasks submittig periodically again.

3. scheduler.scheduleAtFixedDelay: At a proper delay after the completion of the previous task, it will be start executinh at the mentioned delay.

ScheduleFuture<?> scheduleFuture=scheduler.scheduleWithFixedDelay((()-> some task), initial delay, fixeddelay, TimeUnit.SECONDS)

